      "By seven in the morning, all two hundred apple trees on the Lindqvist estate had been picked clean. No ladders had been left behind, no baskets, no boot prints in the wet grass. Only the faint, sweet smell of bruised fruit hung over the orchard like a held breath.",
      "Inspector Marit Holm had smelled it from the lane, before she ever saw the gate. She had wound down the window of the unmarked Volvo somewhere past the church, mostly to let out the stale warmth of the heater, and the air that came in was so thick with apple that she had actually checked the mirror, as though someone might have spilled a crate in the back seat. Beside her, Constable Pelle Aronsson, who was twenty-six and had been awake since four, sniffed too and said it reminded him of his grandmother’s pantry.",
      "“It reminds me of a cider mill,” Marit said. “After the mill has been shut for the night and the pomace is still warm.”",
      "“Is that a good smell or a bad smell?”",
      "“Ask me when I know what it means.”",
      "The lane ran down from the village in a long, slow curve between hedges of hawthorn and dog rose, the hips already red and shining in the thin October light, and at the bottom of the hill it ended at a pair of tall iron gates set into a wall of grey fieldstone. The gates stood open. On the left-hand pillar a brass plate, polished by generations of someone’s patient elbow, read LINDQVIST in capitals gone soft at the edges. Beyond the gates the gravel track divided, one arm running up toward the long yellow manor house with its green shutters and its two chimneys, the other running out between the trees toward a collection of sheds, a cold store and a squat brick building with a tall flue that Marit took to be the old pressing house.",
      "And beyond all of it, on the gentle south-facing slope that fell away toward the lake, lay the orchard.",
      "She had read the file on the way up. Two hundred trees, planted in fourteen long rows by a Lindqvist who had died before the First World War, tended through two wars and a hard post-war decade and a long slow decline, and then, in the last twenty years, quietly restored by the present estate manager and a succession of seasonal hands. Eleven varieties. Some of them she knew from her own childhood, Cox and Gravenstein and the big sour Transparente that went to mush if you looked at it. Others she had never heard of, apples with names like Kejsarinnan and Lille Lars and Fru Astrids Sötäpple, which the file described, in the flat hand of the officer who had taken the first call, as local cultivars of limited commercial value.",
      "Limited commercial value. She looked out over the rows and thought that whoever had written that had not stood where she was standing.",
      "The morning was windless. A low mist lay along the bottom of the slope, thin as muslin, and the trees came up out of it in their ranks, dark-limbed and glistening, every branch hung with beads of dew. In an orchard in the first week of October there ought to have been colour everywhere. There ought to have been red and gold and green-flushed yellow, fruit heavy enough to bend the lower boughs down toward the grass, and the grass itself speckled with the first of the windfalls and loud with wasps. Instead the trees stood quite bare of fruit, though still in leaf, so that the whole slope looked like a company of people who had been dressed for a party and then relieved of their jewellery.",
      "“It’s like somebody’s turned the sound off,” Pelle said. He had stopped beside her with his hands in the pockets of his regulation jacket and his breath smoking in front of his face. “No birds, even.”",
      "“There are birds. They’re just not saying much.” She cocked her head. Far down the slope a blackbird was running through its alarm note, over and over, the dry rattle of a stick dragged along a fence. “Nobody has told them it’s safe.”",
