      "There was a silence in which the blackbird, far down the slope, began again its rattling alarm.",
      "“Then I’m sorry,” said Marit gently, “because that means somebody has done this under your nose, and I can see that it hurts. We’ll start at the beginning. I’d like to see where the contractors were meant to be working this morning, and the gate, and the shed where the apples would have been stored. And I’d like to meet your Mr Lindqvist. In that order, if you don’t mind.”",
      "They walked back up through the rows in the first real sunlight. The mist was burning off the lake now, and the trees threw long shadows westward. The bands of flattened grass were already fading, as she had known they would. She stopped Pelle with a hand on his arm and said quietly, “Photograph those. All of them. Before they’re gone, with something in the frame for scale. A pen, your glove, anything.” He nodded, and ran back down the slope with his phone held out in front of him like a man carrying a bowl of water.",
      "The gate in the stone wall had not been forced. She examined it with some care. The great iron gates stood open now, but the hasp on the left one still held the heavy chain that had secured them, and the chain itself had not been cut. It had been unlocked with a key, the padlock hanging unfastened on one of its links, and when she asked who held the keys, Gustaf told her that there were three sets. One was his. One had belonged to Fru Astrid and now, he supposed, belonged to her nephew. The third hung on a nail in the office of the packing shed, and anyone on the estate could have taken it.",
      "“Anyone on the estate being?”",
      "“Me. Mr Lindqvist, since yesterday. The pickers, but they’re not due until this morning. Karin, who does the books, and she’s on holiday in Gran Canaria. And the boy.” He stopped. “Lasse. He’s nineteen. He drives the tractor in the season. He’s a good boy, I’d vouch for him.”",
      "“We’ll talk to Lasse.”",
      "“He’s at his mother’s in Tomtebo. He came back from the dance on Friday and said he was too tired to start before Monday. He didn’t come up last night.”",
      "“Friday was three days ago.”",
      "“Well. He’s nineteen.”",
      "Pelle had noted all of it down, and Marit, glancing at his page, saw that he had also noted, in the margin, with a small neat arrow, that Mr Ekberg had given the number of his keys without being asked and had then told them the keys were no use as evidence. Good, she thought. He would do.",
      "The Volvo had been joined in the courtyard by a second, larger vehicle, a clean white Mercedes with city plates and a dusting of road salt along its flanks. Beside it, at the foot of the manor steps, a man in his forties was talking into a telephone in a voice that carried right across the gravel.",
      "“No,” he was saying. “No, Henrik, I understand the schedule. I understand that very well. I am telling you that there is, at this moment, no fruit. I am telling you that I am standing in an orchard of two hundred trees and there is not one single apple on any of them. No. I do not think that is funny. Do you hear me laughing?”",
      "He was tall, and somewhat soft about the middle in the way of men who sit in good chairs for a living, and he wore a pale quilted waistcoat of the sort that is sold in the better kind of shop as country clothing. He had Astrid Lindqvist’s long nose, Marit saw, from the portrait in the file, and the same high forehead, though on him it looked less like intellect than impatience. When he saw the two police officers and the manager coming across the gravel he lowered the phone, said, “I will call you back,” and hung up without waiting for an answer.",
      "“Torsten Lindqvist,” he said. He did not offer his hand, perhaps because both of them were occupied with the phone and a set of car keys. “You’re the police?”",
      "“Inspector Marit Holm, from the district office. This is Constable Aronsson. Mr Lindqvist, I’m sorry to meet you under these circumstances. I understand you arrived last night.”",
      "“At about eleven. I was late. There was an accident outside Örebro.” He rubbed his face. “I came in through the back, I let myself in the kitchen door, I had a cold supper and went to bed. I was up at six, I looked out of my window, and I thought I was still dreaming. I went down to the orchard in my slippers.” He glanced down, and Marit saw that he was in fact wearing a pair of expensive leather shoes, scuffed now with damp, and that he seemed to have forgotten changing out of the slippers. “It was like walking into a church after a burglary. Everything just gone.”",
      "“Did you see or hear anything when you arrived? Lights, vehicles, people?”",
      "“No. Nothing. It was dark and quiet, I thought it was marvellous. I remember thinking,” he said, and stopped. “I remember thinking how quiet it was. I haven’t been here since my aunt’s funeral, and before that I hadn’t been here since I was about fourteen. I’d forgotten how quiet it was. Like being inside a cupboard.”",
      "“Your aunt died in April, I understand.”",
      "“The eighteenth of April. A stroke. In the garden, in her chair, with a cup of tea. A very good way to go, everyone told me.” His mouth twisted. “I live in Stockholm, Inspector. I have a firm, two daughters, a mortgage that would make you faint. I did not ask to inherit an apple orchard. But I did, and I have done what I could with it, and what I could do was this. There is a company, Nordfrukt, that makes juice. Good juice, honest juice. They’ve contracted for this harvest, every apple on the estate, a fixed price, delivered to the plant at Jönköping by the fifteenth of the month. Six lorries are coming this morning. Six. They will be here in,” he looked at his watch, “an hour and forty minutes. And I will have nothing to put in them.”",
      "“A fixed price for every apple,” Marit said.",
      "“Every apple. The whole of the harvest.”",
      "“Not just the windfalls and the second-grade fruit.”",
