      "“I’ll want to see that contract.”",
      "“It’s in the office. My lawyer has a copy. I’ll have it sent.”",
      "“Thank you.” Marit looked past him to the great house, with its blind green shutters and its lace-curtained windows, a house that had been closed up for months and was only now beginning to give up its smell of cold ashes and old books to the morning air. She tried to imagine Astrid Lindqvist, whom she had never met and whose portrait showed a long-faced old woman with merry, narrow eyes, sitting in her garden chair in April sunshine with her teacup cooling beside her. “Who else knew you were coming last night?”",
      "“No one. Well. My lawyer. The company. Gustaf, I suppose, since I called him on Thursday to say I would arrive for the pickers.”",
      "“So the company knew when the harvest would be ready, and the lawyer knew, and Mr Ekberg knew,” said Marit, “and the village? How would the village know?”",
      "“I’m sure I don’t know what the village knows,” said Torsten Lindqvist, with a sudden, surprising bitterness. “From what I remember, the village always knew everything. It’s the sort of place where someone sneezes in the post office and a woman three streets away says bless you.”",
      "Gustaf, standing a little behind Marit, shifted his feet on the gravel. It was only a small sound. She heard it all the same.",
      "The packing shed took her another twenty minutes. She walked the length of it twice, with Pelle at her elbow noting what she pointed out. The long benches had been scrubbed. The grading table with its rubber rollers stood in the middle of the floor under a neat canvas cover. Along the far wall rose stacks upon stacks of empty wooden crates, hundreds of them, the old slatted kind, each stamped with the Lindqvist crest of a tree inside a circle, and they had plainly been counted, because a slip of paper tucked into the topmost crate listed the number in a careful round hand. Seven hundred and forty. The pickers had come with their own baskets or none at all. No one had touched the crates. The people who had stripped the orchard overnight had not needed them, or had not wanted to be seen taking them, or had found some other way to move fifty tonnes of apples through the dark.",
      "“Fifty tonnes,” Pelle said, tapping his pencil against his teeth. “You could not carry that in a car boot. You’d need lorries. You’d need a tractor.”",
      "“There’s a tractor,” said Marit. She had seen it in the next building along, a squat blue Valtra with a hay trailer hitched behind, parked nose-out with a thin film of dust on its bonnet. She had put a hand on the engine block and found it cold. “It hasn’t moved in days.”",
      "“Handcarts, then. Wheelbarrows. A human chain.”",
