      "“You think they passed the apples hand to hand?”",
      "“I think nobody at all is going to confess to anything, and I think whoever it was moved fifty tonnes of apples from this hill to somewhere else, before dawn, on foot or on wheels, without leaving a mark. And I think the people who did it are not strangers.” She leaned against the grading table. “Did you notice the sorting?”",
      "“The heaps.”",
      "“The heaps. Think about it, Pelle. If you came to a stranger’s orchard in the dark and wanted to steal as much fruit as possible as quickly as possible, you would not stop to gather up the windfalls. You would certainly not stop to pile them in cones, fourteen of them, all the same size. The people who did this wanted the orchard left in order. They wanted it left tidy. It’s as if they were cleaning up after themselves.”",
      "“After a party.”",
      "“After a harvest festival,” said Marit, and heard what she had said, and smiled a little in spite of everything, because that was exactly how it felt. It did not feel like a crime scene at all. It felt like the morning after a great feast, when the guests have gone and the hosts have swept the floors and tucked the chairs under the tables and gone to bed, and nothing is left but the faint warmth of the room and the smell of what was eaten.",
      "She stood a moment with that thought. Then she shook it off, because she was a police officer and because a man in a quilted waistcoat was about to be sued by a very large juice company, and thinking fondly about the thieves was no help to anyone.",
      "Gustaf had been waiting by the door, saying nothing, with the old collie lying across his feet. She approached him again and asked, without preamble, “Who lives nearest to the orchard?”",
